% Dominators of a function's blocks (docs/plans/plan_prolog.md, stage
% 9), over the facts of mini-ml -flow (Ssa_facts.mli), by what they
% are not: X does not dominate N when N is reached from the entry by a
% path that avoids X. In the squares of a function's blocks: Cooper,
% Harvey and Kennedy's algorithm, which the compiler has, is far less.
%   dom(X, N), sdom(X, N) (strict), idom(X, N) (immediate)
%   back_edge(M, N): to a block that dominates its source, a loop's header
%
% cs-history:
% Reese Prosser named dominance (1959), for a flow diagram's blocks;
% Edward Lowry and C. W. Medlock used it in the Fortran H compiler
% (1969) to find loops. Thomas Lengauer and Robert Tarjan's algorithm
% (1979) is nearly linear and hard to write; Keith Cooper, Timothy
% Harvey and Ken Kennedy's (2001), iterating over the blocks in
% reverse postorder, is a page and as fast in practice: lib_code's
% Dominators. Ron Cytron and others (1991) built SSA on the dominance
% frontiers.
% These rules are the definition, not an algorithm: what the engine
% computes is every pair the definition holds for, quadratic or more;
% they say what the compiler's answer must be, checked against it.
%
% References: Keith Cooper, Timothy Harvey, Ken Kennedy, "A simple,
% fast dominance algorithm" (2001); Thomas Lengauer, Robert Tarjan, "A
% fast algorithm for finding dominators in a flowgraph" (TOPLAS 1979).

avoiding(X, N) :- entry(N), block(N, F), block(X, F), X \= N.
avoiding(X, N) :- avoiding(X, M), succ(M, N), N \= X.
dom(X, N) :- block(X, F), block(N, F), \+ avoiding(X, N).
sdom(X, N) :- dom(X, N), X \= N.
% the immediate one: the strict dominator that every other dominates
not_idom(X, N) :- sdom(X, N), sdom(Y, N), Y \= X, \+ dom(Y, X).
idom(X, N) :- sdom(X, N), \+ not_idom(X, N).
back_edge(M, N) :- succ(M, N), dom(N, M).
